From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: GPU OPP ladder on the GPU0_CLK mux, VDD-GPU
 follows it

Replace the fixed 1008 MHz / 960 mV point with four OPPs and let the driver's
devfreq move between them; make the GPU a cooling device of gpu-thermal.

The OPPs are the four rows of the vendor's GPU table (Allwinner BSP
configs/linux-6.6/sun60iw2p1.dtsi, gpu-opp-table), 400 / 600 / 800 / 1008 MHz at
800 / 800 / 840 / 960 mV, the opp-microvolt-vfdefault column: the one the vendor
uses for a die it cannot bin. Its per-bin columns are lower; until the
following patches hand the speed bin over, the table carries the column that
is safe for every die. No datasheet has an OPP table for this SoC.
Each voltage is inside VDD-GPU's recommended range, 0.80 / 0.80 / 0.96 V (A733
datasheet V1.01, Table 5-2, PDF p. 94).

Each rate is a mux source at M = 0 -- GPUPLL for 1008 MHz, PERI0_800M, 600M and
400M below it -- the same sources the vendor's driver names. No rate comes
from the mask divider (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-move-the-gpu-clock-by-its-mux-only`).

DCDC4 gets the window 800-960 mV: Table 5-2's range and the schematic's for
this rail (v1.10, p. 4, "DCDC4 0.8V-0.96V VDD-GPU"). The rail boots at 800 mV,
inside the window, so nothing is written at PMIC probe.

Start-up order, so that no moment runs a rate above its voltage:
assigned-clocks first switch the mux to PERI0_600M, then set GPUPLL to
1008 MHz once, while the GPU is still unclocked. The driver binds at 600 MHz
on 800 mV, a row of the table and the operating point the vendor stack runs on
this silicon; devfreq then raises the supply before the clock. Without the
driver's devfreq the GPU stays at 600 MHz.

gpu-thermal gets a passive trip at 90 C mapped onto the GPU, the CPU zones'
trip for the CPU zones' reasons (datasheet V1.01 Table 6-2, PDF p. 137: 115 C
continuous; UM V1.00 7.1.1, PDF p. 361: +-3 C sensor error from 60 C).

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
@@ -3072,63 +3072,32 @@
 
 					/*
 					 * DCDC4 = VDD-GPU, volt register 0x15, same
-					 * coding as the two CPU rails above. It gets a
-					 * node -- and therefore a window, and therefore
-					 * the right to be written -- because this series
-					 * also pins its consumer: the GPU node one file
-					 * away asks for 1008 MHz, and 1008 MHz is the row
-					 * of the vendor OPP table that reads 960 mV.
+					 * coding as the two CPU rails above. Its consumer
+					 * is the GPU's OPP table: drm/imagination sets it
+					 * through power-supply with every core clock
+					 * change.
 					 *
-					 * This patch and the 1008 MHz pin are one
-					 * change. Split them and one half is a hazard:
-					 * this rail alone at 960 mV is merely wasteful,
-					 * but the clock alone at 1008 MHz runs the GPU
-					 * under its voltage curve, which is the failure
-					 * mode that does not announce itself.
-					 *
-					 * Order is right by construction: the regulator
-					 * core writes this at PMIC probe, and the GPU's
-					 * rate is applied when powervr probes, seconds
-					 * later. Voltage up first, then clock.
-					 *
-					 * min == max on purpose. This is a FIXED
-					 * operating point, not a ladder: there is no OPP
-					 * table and no devfreq, and drm/powervr reads no
-					 * regulator at all (grepped in the tree we build:
-					 * zero hits for "regulator" and "-supply" under
-					 * drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/). A wider window
-					 * would therefore never be narrowed by anyone --
-					 * a window is enforcement, not a wish.
+					 * Window 800-960 mV: VDD-GPU's recommended range
+					 * (A733 datasheet V1.01, Table 5-2, PDF p. 94:
+					 * 0.80 / 0.80 / 0.96 V) and the schematic's for
+					 * this rail (v1.10, p. 4, "DCDC4 0.8V-0.96V");
+					 * 240 mV under the absolute maximum of 1.2 V
+					 * (Table 5-1, PDF p. 92). The rail boots at
+					 * 800 mV, which is inside, so the regulator core
+					 * writes nothing at PMIC probe; the GPU's clock is
+					 * at PERI0_600M until the OPP core raises the
+					 * rail.
 					 *
 					 * Silicon, read on the 6 GiB board (i2cget -f -y 1 0x36):
 					 *   0x15 = 0x9e -> DVM on, n=30 -> 800 mV
 					 *   0x1b = 0x00 -> poly-phase bits [6]/[7] clear,
 					 *                  so DCDC4+5 are NOT paralleled
 					 *                  and this rail is the GPU's
-					 *                  alone. First measured
-					 *                  2026-07-22, re-read 2026-09-08.
-					 * Windows: schematic rates VDD-GPU 0.8-0.96 V and
-					 * DS Tab. 5-1 gives -0.3..1.2 V absolute, so 960
-					 * mV is the top of the design window and stays
-					 * 240 mV under the limit.
-					 *
-					 * 960 mV itself is not derived from any
-					 * document. No datasheet carries an OPP table for
-					 * this SoC (freq<->voltage comes
-					 * from DT/eFuse and is bin-dependent), and the two
-					 * device trees that do carry one agree with
-					 * each other only on this pair. It is
-					 * the vendor's number, adopted because it is the
-					 * conservative choice among the values and inside
-					 * the documented window -- and it is owed a
-					 * measurement, which a GL render test can give:
-					 * that test fails on wrong PIXELS before the GPU
-					 * hangs, so walking this rail down until that test
-					 * breaks measures the real margin on our silicon.
+					 *                  alone.
 					 */
 					reg_dcdc4: dcdc4 {
 						regulator-name = "vdd-gpu";
-						regulator-min-microvolt = <960000>;
+						regulator-min-microvolt = <800000>;
 						regulator-max-microvolt = <960000>;
 						regulator-ramp-delay = <1280>;
 						regulator-always-on;
@@ -3501,11 +3470,9 @@
 		 *      read 5 = 0x8 and 6 = 0x8 while the vendor driver holds
 		 *      the GPU, against 4 (the NPU) = 0x0 with the NPU idle.
 		 *
-		 * What is deliberately not here yet is any rate control:
-		 * no OPP table, no gpu-supply, no devfreq. This node brings the
-		 * block up at one fixed, vendor-sanctioned operating point and
-		 * nothing more. Frequency and voltage move together or not at
-		 * all, and making them move is its own step.
+		 * Rate control: the core clock and VDD-GPU move together along
+		 * gpu_opp_table (below the node), driven by the driver's
+		 * devfreq; the GPU is also a cooling device of gpu-thermal.
 		 *
 		 * Where the values come from:
 		 *
@@ -3549,81 +3516,33 @@
 			 */
 			power-domains = <&pck600 PD_GPU_TOP>;
 			/*
-			 * The parent is pinned and that is a safety
-			 * property, not a preference. Left alone, GPU0_CLK
-			 * comes out of reset on SEL=000 (GPUPLL) with M=0,
-			 * and PLL_GPU0's own reset value is N=70 P0=2 --
-			 * 840 MHz. Meanwhile VDD-GPU is whatever the boot
-			 * chain left, measured 800 mV on this board, and the
-			 * vendor's own OPP table pairs 800 MHz with 840 mV
-			 * and 1008 MHz with 960 mV. Enabling this node
-			 * without pinning the source therefore runs the GPU
-			 * above the voltage curve, which is the one failure
-			 * mode that does not announce itself.
-			 *
-			 * PERI0_600M with the divider at its reset value is
-			 * exactly the operating point the vendor stack runs
-			 * on this silicon: measured 2026-08-27 under GL load,
-			 * GPU0_CLK = 0x82000000 (SEL=010, M=0) with DCDC4 at
-			 * 800 mV -- and 600 MHz at 800 mV is a row of the
-			 * vendor OPP table, not an extrapolation.
-			 *
-			 * No assigned-clock-rates, deliberately. gpu0
-			 * carries CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT, and its parent here is
-			 * a fixed factor off PLL_PERI0 -- the PLL that feeds
-			 * the whole SoC. A rate request on gpu0 would
-			 * propagate straight into it. Rate control belongs to
-			 * an OPP consumer on the GPU's own PLL, which is the
-			 * next step, not to this line.
-			 *
-			 * And GPUPLL cannot serve 600 MHz cleanly anyway:
-			 * the VCO window is 1.26-2.52 GHz (UM Table 4-2,
-			 * A733 user manual), so 24 MHz x 50 / 2 = 600 needs
-			 * a 1200 MHz VCO, below the floor.
-			 */
-			/*
-			 * 1008 MHz on the GPU's own PLL, which replaces the
-			 * PERI0_600M pin above. The reasoning that put it
-			 * there is unchanged and still true -- the rest of
-			 * the argument has now been established.
+			 * Core clock scaling: drm/imagination's devfreq moves
+			 * the core clock along gpu_opp_table and sets DCDC4
+			 * (VDD-GPU) with it, voltage up before the clock and
+			 * down after it (OPP core).
 			 *
-			 * Why GPUPLL can serve this rate when it could not
-			 * serve 600: the VCO window is 1.272-2.52 GHz (UM
-			 * Table 4-2, and the driver
-			 * enforces it as MIN_VCO/MAX_VCO). 600 MHz needs a
-			 * 1200 MHz VCO, below the floor. 1008 MHz needs
-			 * 24 MHz x 84 = 2016 MHz, comfortably inside it --
-			 * so of the vendor's ladder, only the top row is
-			 * reachable from this PLL at all.
+			 * Each OPP is a source of the GPU0_CLK mux at M = 0:
+			 * GPUPLL for 1008 MHz, PERI0_800M/600M/400M below. The
+			 * CCU offers no masked rate and never re-rates
+			 * PLL_GPU0 on a request (ccu-sun60iw2.c, GPU block),
+			 * so a transition is one glitch-free mux switch (UM
+			 * V1.00 GPU0_CLK_REG, PDF p. 148) and no PLL relock.
 			 *
-			 * One rate request, and the model has exactly one
-			 * legal answer. gpu0 carries CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT
-			 * with CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT, and gpupll carries
-			 * CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT, so 1008 MHz propagates:
-			 * gpu0 mask M=0 (/1) -> gpupll 1008 -> P0 must be 2,
-			 * because P0=1 would ask the VCO for 1008 MHz and
-			 * the enforced floor refuses it. VCO 2016, P0 2,
-			 * M 0. Nothing here is a magic number; every step is
-			 * the only one the window permits.
-			 *
-			 * The warning of `arm64-dts-a733-enable-the-gpu-at-the-vendor-s-operating-point` about CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT does
-			 * not transfer, and that is the whole reason this is
-			 * now safe: it warned that a rate on gpu0 would
-			 * propagate into PLL_PERI0, the PLL feeding the whole
-			 * SoC. With GPUPLL as the parent the request
-			 * propagates into the GPU's own PLL, which drives
-			 * nothing else -- "mem" is that same PLL and the
-			 * driver only clk_prepare_enable()s it, never sets a
-			 * rate on it (`arm64-dts-a733-gpu-mem-clock-is-the-gpu-pll`).
-			 *
-			 * And it is only safe with the DCDC4 node in the
-			 * same series. 1008 MHz at the 800 mV this rail
-			 * boots with is an under-volt, and an under-volt does
-			 * not announce itself.
+			 * assigned-clocks run before the driver binds, with
+			 * the GPU still unclocked: the mux goes to PERI0_600M
+			 * first, then GPUPLL is set to 1008 MHz once (24 MHz x
+			 * 84 = 2016 MHz VCO, inside UM Table 4-2's window, P0 =
+			 * 2). It is never re-rated after that. The GPU starts
+			 * at 600 MHz because that rate is safe at the 800 mV
+			 * DCDC4 boots with: if the driver never brings up
+			 * devfreq, this is where the GPU stays.
 			 */
-			assigned-clocks = <&ccu CLK_GPU0>;
-			assigned-clock-parents = <&ccu CLK_GPUPLL>;
-			assigned-clock-rates = <1008000000>;
+			assigned-clocks = <&ccu CLK_GPU0>, <&ccu CLK_GPUPLL>;
+			assigned-clock-parents = <&ccu CLK_PLL_PERI0_600M>, <0>;
+			assigned-clock-rates = <0>, <1008000000>;
+			operating-points-v2 = <&gpu_opp_table>;
+			power-supply = <&reg_dcdc4>;
+			#cooling-cells = <2>;
 			status = "okay";
 		};
 	};
@@ -3631,6 +3550,51 @@
 
 / {
 	/*
+	 * GPU OPPs: the four rows of the vendor's GPU table (Allwinner BSP
+	 * configs/linux-6.6/sun60iw2p1.dtsi, gpu-opp-table) at their
+	 * opp-microvolt-vfdefault values, the column the vendor applies when it
+	 * cannot place a die in a bin. The vendor's per-bin columns are lower
+	 * (down to 860 mV at 1008 MHz); this tree has no way to hand a bin to
+	 * the GPU's OPP table, so it carries the column that is safe for every
+	 * die.
+	 *
+	 * Every voltage lies in VDD-GPU's recommended range, 0.80-0.96 V (A733
+	 * datasheet V1.01, Table 5-2, PDF p. 94), the same window the board's
+	 * schematic gives DCDC4 (v1.10, p. 4: "DCDC4 0.8V-0.96V VDD-GPU").
+	 *
+	 * Each rate is a GPU0_CLK mux source at M = 0, a clock of that period
+	 * -- the vendor's driver lists the same PERI0 taps as its rate sources.
+	 * Rates from the mask divider are not used (ccu-sun60iw2.c, GPU block):
+	 * a masked rate needs the voltage of its source, not of its average.
+	 * 600 MHz at 800 mV is also the operating point the vendor stack runs
+	 * on this silicon, and 1008 MHz at 960 mV the one this tree pinned
+	 * before it had a ladder.
+	 */
+	gpu_opp_table: opp-table-gpu {
+		compatible = "operating-points-v2";
+
+		opp-400000000 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <400000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <800000>;
+		};
+
+		opp-600000000 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <600000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <800000>;
+		};
+
+		opp-800000000 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <800000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <840000>;
+		};
+
+		opp-1008000000 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1008000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <960000>;
+		};
+	};
+
+	/*
 	 * A55 cluster (cpu0..cpu5), supplied by DCDC5 = VDD-CPUL.
 	 *
 	 * Frequencies are 26 MHz * N with the N values read out of
@@ -4453,13 +4417,12 @@
  * C, and this tree gains it in `arm64-dts-a733-idle-injection-cooling`.
  * Until that lands, the 20 C between 90 and 110 has nothing in it.
  *
- * Throttling is wired for both clusters and is the only cooling this
- * tree can offer today: cpufreq-dt owns the CPU-big and CPU-little
- * policies (`arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` and
- * `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply`), and there is no devfreq
- * for DDR, NPU or GPU. Those three therefore carry a critical trip and
- * no cooling map -- an emergency shutdown is real protection, an
- * unwired passive trip would only look like it.
+ * Throttling is wired for both clusters: cpufreq-dt owns the CPU-big
+ * and CPU-little policies (`arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` and
+ * `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply`). The GPU is throttled by
+ * its own devfreq (drm/imagination) from gpu-thermal's passive trip,
+ * the same 90 C as the CPU zones for the same reasons. There is no
+ * devfreq for DDR or NPU.
  */
 &cpu0 {
 	#cooling-cells = <2>;
@@ -4700,6 +4663,19 @@
 					type = "critical";
 					hysteresis = <0>;
 				};
+				/* Same passive trip as the CPU zones, see above. */
+				gpu_passive: gpu-trip-1 {
+					temperature = <90000>;
+					type = "passive";
+					hysteresis = <2000>;
+				};
+			};
+
+			cooling-maps {
+				map0 {
+					trip = <&gpu_passive>;
+					cooling-device = <&gpu THERMAL_NO_LIMIT THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
+				};
 			};
 		};
 	};
